# REQ-013: Coding

**Priority:** Must Have (manual coding); Should Have (text-selection shortcut; practice-configured dictionary suggestions)
**Effort:** Low (manual coding — pre-built ✅); Medium (text-selection pre-population — net-new, low complexity); Medium (practice dictionary config — net-new)
**Risk:** Low (manual coding; text selection); Medium (dictionary suggestions — must require user confirmation; practice-controlled)
**Depends On:** REQ-012 (Filing), REQ-004 (Document Viewer & Navigation), REQ-009 (Audit Trail)

> ⚠️ **Implementation note:**
> - **Manual code entry** is handled by the pre-built **Code Picker** component (EMIS-X Terminology Service). **Do not re-implement.** Integrate the existing component.
> - **Text-selection pre-population** (user selects text in the PDF viewer → pre-populated into Code Picker search box) and **practice-configured dictionary suggestions** are the only net-new engineering work in this requirement.
> - **AI automated code suggestions are out of scope for Documents Management Core** and have been removed from this requirement. Deferred to Phase 2.
> - **Cross-queue embedding:** The coding panel must be embeddable as a secondary action in the Filing Queue context (see REQ-001 cross-queue action availability model). The same Code Picker component, mandatory confirmation requirements, and validation logic apply. The coding panel is gated on patient match/filing being confirmed first.

---

## User Story

As a practice administrator or coder,
I need to add clinical codes to a patient record based on the content of an inbound document, with tools that make it efficient to locate and apply the right SNOMED codes from the document text and from practice-configured code mappings,
So that the patient record is accurately coded without unnecessary friction, and all coding decisions remain under the user's explicit control.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] Users can search for and add SNOMED codes to the patient's clinical consultation record using the Code Picker (EMIS-X Terminology Service). Codes committed via the Code Picker are captured in the consultation as codes for the patient's care record. *(Must Have — pre-built)*
- [ ] Code search supports free-text input in addition to term and code-based lookup. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Multiple codes can be added to a patient record from a single document. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Users can add, review, and remove codes from a staging list before committing them to the patient record. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] For each selected clinical code, users can mark whether it is a **Problem** code and set significance as **Minor**, **Significant**, or **Major**. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] For each selected code, users can add an optional comment before commit. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] The coding panel includes observations as part of the coding workflow, with explicit user confirmation before commit. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Clinical codes that are QOF-relevant display a QOF indicator in the coding UI. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] The code section displays existing problem codes for the patient to provide context before adding new codes. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] All coding actions (add, remove, commit) are audited. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Documents are rendered as PDF in the document viewer, enabling text selection. Selecting text in the viewer pre-populates the Code Picker search box with the selected text, initiating a SNOMED search. *(Should Have)*
- [ ] Double-clicking a single word in the document viewer pre-populates the Code Picker search box with that word. *(Should Have)*
- [ ] Practice administrators can configure a dictionary mapping text strings to specific SNOMED codes. *(Should Have)*
- [ ] When one or more dictionary-configured text strings are identified in the document, the corresponding SNOMED codes are surfaced as coding suggestions for the user. *(Should Have)*
- [ ] Dictionary-based coding suggestions are never auto-committed to the patient record; the user must explicitly accept each suggestion. *(Must Have — applies when dictionary suggestions are present)*
- [ ] Dictionary-based suggestions are clearly labelled as dictionary-matched, distinguishing them from codes found via manual search. *(Should Have)*
- [ ] A staged code can be accepted, modified, or removed individually before final commit. *(Must Have)*

